WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.415
Lesson zero point five.

00:00:02.415 --> 00:00:04.710
Setting up your wallet and backing it up.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.850
By the end, you'll install a wallet

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.405
safely, back up the seed phrase correctly,

00:00:09.405 --> 00:00:11.545
and prove the backup actually works.

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:16.250
Here's why this lesson gets its own dedicated time.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.721
Almost every story of someone losing everything traces

00:00:19.721 --> 00:00:22.245
back to this exact setup moment: a fake

00:00:22.245 --> 00:00:25.148
app, a backup that was never actually tested,

00:00:25.148 --> 00:00:28.035
a seed phrase typed into the wrong place.

00:00:28.035 --> 00:00:30.614
Slow down here, and you close off nearly all

00:00:30.614 --> 00:00:32.963
of it before you ever hold a real amount.

00:00:34.263 --> 00:00:35.553
Here's the plan.

00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:38.638
First, software wallets versus hardware wallets.

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:41.023
Second, the five-step safe install.

00:00:41.023 --> 00:00:43.818
Third, a scam you need to recognise on sight.

00:00:43.818 --> 00:00:45.776
Fourth, backing up correctly, and

00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:48.033
proving the backup actually works.

00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:50.416
And finally, Sam's real worked example,

00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:51.965
your checklist and a quiz.

00:00:53.265 --> 00:00:54.430
Two kinds of wallet.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:58.906
A software wallet, a browser extension or a phone

00:00:58.906 --> 00:01:02.510
app, like MetaMask or Rabby, is free and convenient.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.115
Your keys live on your computer or phone.

00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:08.150
A hardware wallet, a small physical device, like

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.690
Ledger or Trezor, keeps your keys on the

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.035
device itself, and every transaction must be approved

00:01:14.035 --> 00:01:16.255
right there, on its own screen.

00:01:16.255 --> 00:01:18.405
It's recommended once you're holding more

00:01:18.405 --> 00:01:19.839
than you'd be upset to lose.

00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:22.518
One rule that matters more

00:01:22.518 --> 00:01:24.044
than the brand you pick.

00:01:24.044 --> 00:01:26.180
Buy a hardware wallet only from the

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.741
manufacturer's own official website, never second-hand, and

00:01:29.741 --> 00:01:31.999
never from a marketplace listing.

00:01:31.999 --> 00:01:34.505
A used or third-party device can arrive

00:01:34.505 --> 00:01:37.011
tampered with, in ways you'd never spot

00:01:37.011 --> 00:01:38.389
just by looking at it.

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:42.904
Here's exactly why a hardware wallet protects you, even

00:01:42.904 --> 00:01:46.304
if the computer it's plugged into is already infected.

00:01:46.304 --> 00:01:49.407
Your computer prepares a transaction, but it can't

00:01:49.407 --> 00:01:52.079
sign it, only the device holds the key.

00:01:52.079 --> 00:01:54.611
The device shows you the real destination

00:01:54.611 --> 00:01:56.962
and amount, on its own trusted screen,

00:01:56.962 --> 00:01:58.950
one the malware can't touch.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.505
You check that screen, not your computer's.

00:02:01.505 --> 00:02:03.229
And you approve or reject right

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:04.845
there, with a physical button.

00:02:06.144 --> 00:02:07.254
The safe install.

00:02:08.554 --> 00:02:11.634
Step one: get the wallet from its official website.

00:02:11.634 --> 00:02:14.122
Type the address yourself, or use the link

00:02:14.122 --> 00:02:16.726
on the official site, since fake wallet apps

00:02:16.726 --> 00:02:19.156
and sponsored search ads are a common way

00:02:19.156 --> 00:02:22.234
beginners get tricked from the very first click.

00:02:22.234 --> 00:02:25.056
Step two: create a brand new wallet, never one

00:02:25.056 --> 00:02:27.998
that came from someone else, or pre-printed in a

00:02:27.998 --> 00:02:30.159
box, a scam we'll walk through next.

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:34.222
Step three: write the seed phrase on paper, or

00:02:34.222 --> 00:02:37.284
stamp it into metal, in order, spelled exactly.

00:02:37.284 --> 00:02:40.293
Step four: store it somewhere private, safe from

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:43.057
fire and water, and for larger amounts, keep

00:02:43.057 --> 00:02:45.629
a second copy in a separate location.

00:02:45.629 --> 00:02:48.568
Step five: never store it in photos, email,

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:51.975
notes apps, cloud drives, or password managers, no

00:02:51.975 --> 00:02:55.114
matter how convenient that feels in the moment.

00:02:56.414 --> 00:02:59.091
Here's exactly why “just this once” with

00:02:59.091 --> 00:03:01.349
a cloud photo is so dangerous.

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:03.147
You photograph the seed phrase,

00:03:03.147 --> 00:03:04.964
meaning to delete it later.

00:03:04.964 --> 00:03:06.804
It syncs automatically to every

00:03:06.804 --> 00:03:08.714
device tied to that account.

00:03:08.714 --> 00:03:10.586
That account gets phished, or its

00:03:10.586 --> 00:03:13.199
password gets reused and leaked elsewhere.

00:03:13.199 --> 00:03:15.382
And whoever gets in doesn't just see a

00:03:15.382 --> 00:03:18.294
photo, they see your entire wallet, instantly, with

00:03:18.294 --> 00:03:20.309
nothing left for you to do about it.

00:03:21.609 --> 00:03:24.109
One nuance on paper versus metal.

00:03:24.109 --> 00:03:26.918
Paper is free and fine for most people, but

00:03:26.918 --> 00:03:30.154
it can burn, soak through, or fade over years.

00:03:30.154 --> 00:03:32.653
Metal, a stamped plate, costs more, but

00:03:32.653 --> 00:03:34.715
survives a house fire or a flood

00:03:34.715 --> 00:03:37.139
that would destroy paper outright.

00:03:37.139 --> 00:03:39.672
Neither replaces having a second copy in

00:03:39.672 --> 00:03:42.451
a separate location; that habit matters more

00:03:42.451 --> 00:03:44.366
than which material you choose.

00:03:45.666 --> 00:03:46.756
Quick check.

00:03:46.756 --> 00:03:48.941
Why get the wallet app from the official

00:03:48.941 --> 00:03:52.031
website specifically, rather than the first search result?

00:03:56.451 --> 00:03:58.620
The answer: sponsored search ads and

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.561
fake apps are a common scam.

00:04:00.561 --> 00:04:02.767
Typing the address yourself avoids that

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:04.311
trap at the very first step.

00:04:05.611 --> 00:04:07.421
A scam to recognise on sight.

00:04:08.721 --> 00:04:10.916
Here's exactly how this scam works.

00:04:10.916 --> 00:04:13.539
You buy what looks like a genuine hardware

00:04:13.539 --> 00:04:17.132
wallet, sometimes even from a real-looking store page.

00:04:17.132 --> 00:04:18.970
Inside the box is a card with

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:22.095
twenty-four words already printed on it, presented

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:24.417
as your seed phrase, ready to go.

00:04:24.417 --> 00:04:26.882
You fund the wallet, trusting those words.

00:04:26.882 --> 00:04:29.104
And the scammer, who wrote down that

00:04:29.104 --> 00:04:31.565
same seed phrase before ever selling you

00:04:31.565 --> 00:04:34.027
the box, drains it, often within minutes.

00:04:35.327 --> 00:04:37.054
Here's the one fact that makes

00:04:37.054 --> 00:04:39.082
this scam avoidable every time.

00:04:39.082 --> 00:04:42.720
A genuine device generates your seed phrase itself, fresh,

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.186
the first time you set it up, and shows

00:04:45.186 --> 00:04:47.582
it to you once, on its own screen.

00:04:47.582 --> 00:04:50.443
If words are already printed anywhere in the

00:04:50.443 --> 00:04:52.604
box, on a card, a sticker, a slip

00:04:52.604 --> 00:04:55.447
of paper, that box has been compromised.

00:04:55.447 --> 00:04:56.632
Don't use it.

00:04:56.632 --> 00:04:59.837
Return it, and buy again from the manufacturer directly.

00:05:01.137 --> 00:05:02.462
Backing it up correctly.

00:05:03.762 --> 00:05:06.092
Here's the do-and-don't in one picture.

00:05:06.092 --> 00:05:07.947
Do: paper or metal, private,

00:05:07.947 --> 00:05:09.847
safe from fire and water.

00:05:09.847 --> 00:05:13.578
Don't: photos, email, notes apps, cloud drives, password

00:05:13.578 --> 00:05:16.917
managers, anything connected to the internet, ever.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:21.418
And here's the step almost every beginner skips: proving

00:05:21.418 --> 00:05:25.032
the backup actually works, before there's anything at risk.

00:05:25.032 --> 00:05:27.856
Remove the wallet, or use a second device.

00:05:27.856 --> 00:05:30.081
Choose restore, not create new.

00:05:30.081 --> 00:05:33.016
Restore with your written backup words, in order.

00:05:33.016 --> 00:05:35.775
And check that the exact same address appears.

00:05:35.775 --> 00:05:37.915
If it does, your backup is real.

00:05:37.915 --> 00:05:39.520
If it doesn't, you've just found a

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.080
mistake while it cost you nothing.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:45.211
Here's what a restore test actually catches, that

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:47.775
just glancing at your paper never would.

00:05:47.775 --> 00:05:49.870
A word written down slightly wrong.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:51.498
Two words swapped in order,

00:05:51.498 --> 00:05:53.425
easy to do without noticing.

00:05:53.425 --> 00:05:55.129
A word that's hard to read back

00:05:55.129 --> 00:05:57.985
later, your own handwriting working against you.

00:05:57.985 --> 00:06:00.162
Every one of these breaks the restore

00:06:00.162 --> 00:06:02.282
completely, and every one of them is

00:06:02.282 --> 00:06:04.345
invisible until you actually try it.

00:06:05.645 --> 00:06:06.735
Quick check.

00:06:06.735 --> 00:06:08.409
Why do this restore test with

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:09.860
nothing in the wallet yet?

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.718
The answer: to prove your written backup is

00:06:16.718 --> 00:06:19.345
correct while nothing is actually at risk.

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:20.631
If the backup is wrong,

00:06:20.631 --> 00:06:21.810
you find out for free.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:24.972
And one reminder from earlier in

00:06:24.972 --> 00:06:27.190
this module, worth repeating here.

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:31.157
Your seed phrase's words come from a standard

00:06:31.157 --> 00:06:35.510
list of 2,048, exactly like Lesson 0.0 covered.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.958
That's also why a restore test with even

00:06:37.958 --> 00:06:41.003
one wrong word fails completely: there's no "close

00:06:41.003 --> 00:06:43.750
enough" with a system built to be unguessable.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:48.661
One more question worth answering honestly, even though this

00:06:48.661 --> 00:06:52.095
program stops short of full estate planning: what happens

00:06:52.095 --> 00:06:55.060
to this backup if something happens to you?

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.081
A written seed phrase that only you

00:06:57.081 --> 00:06:59.327
know the location of can be permanently

00:06:59.327 --> 00:07:01.480
lost to the people who'd need it.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.908
A brief, trusted note about where it lives,

00:07:03.908 --> 00:07:06.613
kept separately from the phrase itself, is worth

00:07:06.613 --> 00:07:09.010
more thought than most people give it.

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:10.958
And none of this needs to happen

00:07:10.958 --> 00:07:13.911
in one rushed sitting either: installing, writing

00:07:13.911 --> 00:07:16.095
the backup, and the restore test can

00:07:16.095 --> 00:07:18.339
each take their own quiet ten minutes.

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:20.979
Sam's worked example.

00:07:22.279 --> 00:07:24.344
Here's exactly what Sam does.

00:07:24.344 --> 00:07:26.879
Installs Rabby, from its official site.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:29.342
Writes the 12 words onto the blank card

00:07:29.342 --> 00:07:32.052
that came in the hardware wallet box, since

00:07:32.052 --> 00:07:34.939
a genuine box's card always arrives blank.

00:07:34.939 --> 00:07:38.118
Removes the wallet from the computer, and restores it

00:07:38.118 --> 00:07:41.362
on a laptop instead, using only the written words.

00:07:41.362 --> 00:07:46.817
Sees the exact same address appear: 0x4b...e1.

00:07:46.817 --> 00:07:49.166
And only then, with the restore already

00:07:49.166 --> 00:07:51.222
proven, sends any real money to it.

00:07:52.522 --> 00:07:54.953
Notice the detail that made this safe:

00:07:54.953 --> 00:07:57.497
the card in Sam's box arrived blank.

00:07:57.497 --> 00:07:59.998
That's exactly what a genuine device does,

00:07:59.998 --> 00:08:02.093
it generates the words with you, it

00:08:02.093 --> 00:08:04.487
never hands them to you pre-written.

00:08:04.487 --> 00:08:07.004
That one blank card is the whole tell, and

00:08:07.004 --> 00:08:09.813
it's the same check worth making the moment any

00:08:09.813 --> 00:08:13.032
hardware wallet box is opened, regardless of the brand.

00:08:14.332 --> 00:08:15.512
Checklist and quiz.

00:08:16.812 --> 00:08:18.707
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:08:18.707 --> 00:08:21.287
Wallet installed from the official website.

00:08:21.287 --> 00:08:23.056
New seed phrase written on

00:08:23.056 --> 00:08:25.322
paper or metal, never digital.

00:08:25.322 --> 00:08:28.247
Restore test done, and the same address appeared.

00:08:28.247 --> 00:08:30.532
And a hardware wallet planned for larger

00:08:30.532 --> 00:08:32.762
amounts, bought directly from the maker.

00:08:34.062 --> 00:08:35.332
Question three. A

00:08:35.332 --> 00:08:37.657
wallet box arrives with a card already

00:08:37.657 --> 00:08:39.685
showing 24 words. What do you

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:40.535
do? [[pause 4]]

00:08:44.955 --> 00:08:46.750
The answer: don't use it.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:47.920
It's a scam.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.901
A genuine device generates your seed phrase itself,

00:08:50.901 --> 00:08:53.080
and never hands it to you pre-printed.

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:55.635
Question four.

00:08:55.635 --> 00:08:57.970
Is a photo of your seed phrase, stored

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:00.065
in your cloud drive, a safe backup?

00:09:04.485 --> 00:09:06.416
The answer: no. Anyone who gets

00:09:06.416 --> 00:09:09.010
into that account can take everything.

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:12.055
Paper or metal, offline, is the only safe form.

00:09:13.355 --> 00:09:16.165
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:16.165 --> 00:09:18.205
Install from the official site.

00:09:18.205 --> 00:09:19.720
Write the seed phrase on

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:21.960
paper or metal, never digitally.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.695
Prove the restore works, with nothing at risk.

00:09:24.695 --> 00:09:26.240
Only then, fund it.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:28.164
And for larger amounts, move to a

00:09:28.164 --> 00:09:31.180
hardware wallet, bought directly from the maker.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.635
Do those five things, and this lesson is done.

00:09:34.935 --> 00:09:37.980
This is education, not financial advice.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.216
Set up your own wallet carefully and

00:09:40.216 --> 00:09:42.271
at your own pace, and nobody from

00:09:42.271 --> 00:09:44.447
this program will ever ask for your

00:09:44.447 --> 00:09:47.106
seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:09:48.406 --> 00:09:50.871
That's setting up your wallet and backing it up.

00:09:50.871 --> 00:09:53.188
Install it safely, back it up on paper,

00:09:53.188 --> 00:09:55.921
and prove the restore before you trust it.

00:09:55.921 --> 00:09:58.055
Next up, Lesson zero point six:

00:09:58.055 --> 00:10:00.656
networks, gas, and your first transfer.
